I had the same issue once - here's the explanation as far as I understand:
- Server action throws a custom error (or any error with predefined properties).
- The error thrown is converted to an RSC payload and transmitted over the network to the client.
- The custom properties that were added are simply ignored - only the error name and stack trace is sent to the client (stack trace is only sent in dev mode)
- The only custom property that nextjs sends with the error is named `digest`.

This is not an ideal approach, but I use this `digest` property to serialize and de-serialize my custom errors on the client. Here's an example:

```ts
export class MyError extends Error {
  prop: number
  digest: string

  constructor(msg: string) {
    super(msg)
    this.prop = 123
    this.digest = ''
  }

  // can also add some type discriminator to make it easy to deserialize
  serializeToError() {
    this.digest = JSON.stringify({ message: this.message, prop: this.prop })
    return this
  }

  // you can make this function more robust
  static deserialize(digest: string) {
    const { message, prop } = JSON.parse(digest)
    const error = new MyError(message)
    error.prop = prop
    return error
  }
}
```

Then in server action:
```ts
export async function serverAction2() {
  const error = new MyError('Message')
  throw error.serializeToError()
}
```

In the component:
```ts
try {
  await serverAction2();
} catch (error: any) {
  const myErr = MyError.deserialize(error.digest);
}
```

