Try it out here, https://stackblitz.com/edit/github-kmjdpvry?file=app%2Fapi%2Femployee-shifts%2F[id]%2Froute.ts

```tsx
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export async function GET(
  _req: NextRequest,
  context: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }
) {
  const { id } = await context.params;
  return NextResponse.json({
    id,
    message: 'Hello from dynamic API route!',
  });
}
```

This is how it has to be. Think of it this way, Next.js is in charge of eventually invoking `GET`.

What they are telling you is that, when they do call it, they'll pass a `params` object, that's a Promise. The build step is just ensuring that your function definition aligns with that, otherwise, we know we might be allowing an incorrect build, that could break at runtime.

They have a compatibility layer in v15, so it kind of works in some cases, but the recommendation is to just assume the dynamic promise access like.

