I think you did not follow completely.

First of all this code (which uses middleware) is straight from [documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/authentication#optimistic-checks-with-middleware-optional):

```
export default async function middleware(req: NextRequest) {
  // 2. Check if the current route is protected or public
  const path = req.nextUrl.pathname
  const isProtectedRoute = protectedRoutes.includes(path)
  const isPublicRoute = publicRoutes.includes(path)

  // 3. Decrypt the session from the cookie
  const cookie = (await cookies()).get('session')?.value
  const session = await decrypt(cookie)

  // 4. Redirect to /login if the user is not authenticated
  if (isProtectedRoute && !session?.userId) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', req.nextUrl))
  }

  // 5. Redirect to /dashboard if the user is authenticated
  if (
    isPublicRoute &&
    session?.userId &&
    !req.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith('/dashboard')
  ) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/dashboard', req.nextUrl))
  }

  return NextResponse.next()
}
```

So they actually do suggest to use middleware in some cases.

But like I said this code can be problematic when the middleware is invoked due to server function. Even if you have checks inside the server function, the problem is that due to above code (namely due to "redirect"), the server function which triggered this middleware **is not even invoked** and returns `undefined` - and the redirect itself also does not work properly in such case. Please check [here](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/64993) again for more details.

So the point is documentation _should at least mention this_ and suggest solution like to configure the middleware (as in the gh issue) such that it ignores server functions, otherwise it can lead to bugs.

Encountering this too. I mitigated it by adding extra checks in NextJS's `middleware.(j|t)s`

```ts
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  if (request.method === 'POST') {
    const contentType = request.headers.get('content-type')
    // There is a bug in Next.js where the framework tries to parse the body without checking if the body is empty,
    // If multipart/form-data and empty body, return 400
    // See https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/81760
    if (contentType?.startsWith('multipart/form-data')) {
      const contentLength = request.headers.get('content-length')
      if (!contentLength || contentLength === '0') {
        return new NextResponse(undefined, { status: 400 })
      }
    }
  }
  return NextResponse.next()
}
```

Does not cover all edge cases, just the case where the form data is empty, but that is the majority of our errors throwing 5xx now.
but realistically this should be fixed on NextJS's end.

