Hi,

This doesn't appear to be a Next.js bug and is an issue with your `mdx-components.tsx` configuration. When MDX processes text with embedded JSX expressions like `{siteInfo.name}`, it creates an array of React children. The original component was using `String(children)` which converts React arrays to strings with commas between elements, causing artifacts like `,Startup Template,`.

You'll need to update your paragraph component in `mdx-components.tsx` to properly handle mixed content. For example:

```jsx
p: ({ children }) => {
  const childrenArray = React.Children.toArray(children)
  const hasMixedContent = childrenArray.length > 1 || 
    childrenArray.some(child => React.isValidElement(child))

  if (hasMixedContent) {
    return (
      <p style={{ marginBottom: '16px' }}>
        {childrenArray.map((child, index) => {
          if (typeof child === 'string') {
            return <span key={index}>{child}</span>
          }
          return <span key={index}>{child}</span>
        })}
      </p>
    )
  }

  // Handle simple text content as before
  return <p style={{ marginBottom: '16px' }}>{children}</p>
}
```

The key is to map over each child individually instead of stringifying the entire children array.

