@mjy9088 Oh shoot, my bad! 😅 I led you down the wrong path there. You can't have nested `<html>` and `<body>` tags - that's exactly what's causing that hydration error you're seeing.

Here's what I should have said:

```
// src/app/layout.tsx (ONLY this file gets <html> and <body>)
export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}

// src/app/[locale]/layout.tsx (No HTML tags here!)
'use client';
import { useEffect } from 'react';

export default function LocaleLayout({
  children,
  params,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  params: { locale: string };
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Just update the existing <html> element's lang attribute
    document.documentElement.lang = params.locale;
  }, [params.locale]);

  return <>{children}</>;
}
```

The rule is simple: **only your root layout** (`src/app/layout.tsx`) should have the `<html>` and `<body>` tags. All other layouts just wrap content in divs or fragments.

Sorry for the confusion earlier! This should fix that hydration error you're getting. The `useEffect` will dynamically set the lang attribute on the existing HTML element without creating duplicate tags.

