When you say:

> The Next.js build should complete successfully, as the DELETE handler's signature conforms to the official App Router documentation for dynamic routes.

in 

```ts
interface RouteParams {
  id: string;
}

// This is the standard, documented signature for a dynamic DELETE route handler.
// In Next.js v15.3.0 (and recent canaries), this signature incorrectly
// causes the build to fail.
export async function DELETE(
  request: NextRequest,
  context: { params: RouteParams }
) {
  // The logic here doesn't matter for the bug, but we return a success response.
  return NextResponse.json({ success: true, id: context.params.id });
}
```

That's not correct, and it is not documented that way either. 

- https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/route#context-optional

```ts
// This is what's documented
export async function GET(
  request: Request,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ team: string }> }
) {
  const { team } = await params
}
```

This has also been communicated since the release of v15: 

- https://nextjs.org/blog/next-15#async-request-apis-breaking-change
- https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/upgrading/version-15#route-handlers

There's a compatibility layer, which mostly just works for JavaScript, but one should use the async request APIs syntax:

```ts
export async function DELETE(
  request: NextRequest,
  context: { params: Promise<RouteParams> }
) {
  // The logic here doesn't matter for the bug, but we return a success response.
  const { id } = await context.params
  return NextResponse.json({ success: true, id });
}
```

