# Quick Fix for Node Runtime Middleware `onRequestError`

Hi everyone,  

I also encountered the issue where `Instrumentation.onRequestError` doesn’t catch errors in Node runtime middleware, and I managed to fix it.  

The problem is that in `NextNodeServer.runMiddleware`, the `try…finally` block runs the middleware but doesn’t catch errors:

```javascript
try {
    result = await adapterFn({ /* ... */ });
} finally {
    if (hasRequestBody) requestData.body.finalize();
}
```

Adding a `catch` ensures errors are logged via `onRequestError / Instrumentation`:

```javascript
try {
    result = await adapterFn({ /* ... */ });
} catch (err) {

        await this.instrumentationOnRequestError(err, params.request, {
        routePath: params.request.url || '/middleware',
        routerKind: 'Pages Router',
        routeType: 'middleware',
        // middleware is not part of ISR logic
        revalidateReason: undefined,
        })

    throw err;
} finally {
    if (hasRequestBody) requestData.body.finalize();
}
```
Otherwise errors will be catched / printed out nicely in the dev-server (without Instrumentation hooks) and cannot be logged by Instrumentation (production/dev).
Console now shows this (also in Node runtime):

```
catch error = Error: middleware error
 ⨯ Error: middleware error
    at middleware (src\middleware.ts:4:9)
  2 |
  3 | export const middleware = (request: NextRequest) => {
> 4 |   throw new Error('middleware error');
    |         ^
  5 | };
  6 |
  7 | export const config: MiddlewareConfig = {
```

This aligns Node runtime behavior with Edge runtime.  

Since I’m still relatively new and learning, I’d love any feedback on this approach—whether there’s a cleaner way to handle it or improvements I could make.

