Confirming this still hits Next.js 15.5.x in production. PR #85418 was merged to canary in Nov 2025 but wasn't backported to 15.x, so anyone still on the 15.5 line is exposed indefinitely. We're seeing the same `TypeError: Response body object should not be disturbed or locked` on multipart file uploads in the 7–30MB range, intermittently, under production load on Next.js 15.5.11.

Couldn't reproduce it in isolation — ran a 45+ cell matrix of `runtime: 'nodejs'` middleware × `NextResponse.next({ request: { headers } })` × dynamic `[param]` route × `formData()` / `arrayBuffer()` / `json()` consumption × `params`-first / body-first await ordering. All green in dev and prod with sequential 1MB requests. Even 200×30-parallel 1MB requests passed locally. Consistent with #86373's "intermittent on medium payloads under load" framing — the race window is too narrow for a synthetic repro on dev hardware. Anyone trying to reproduce in isolation will likely hit the same wall.

### Workaround that resolves it deterministically: matcher-exclude body-forwarding routes from the middleware

```ts
// middleware.ts
export const config = {
  runtime: 'nodejs',
  matcher: [
    '/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon\\.ico|api/uploads/|api/proxy/|api/file-forwarding-routes/).*)',
  ],
};
```

Auth that previously ran in middleware moves to a per-route helper that reads the same cookie or `Authorization` header. Since middleware never runs for the excluded paths, `cloneBodyStream()` and `finalize()` are never called, and the race fundamentally can't happen for those routes. This is a deterministic fix at the application layer — no Next.js patch required, and it works on 15.x where the upstream fix was never backported.

The trade-off is per-route auth derivation instead of one centralized middleware check. For us that was acceptable because the affected paths (file uploads, body-forwarding proxies) already have route-specific concerns that are easier to express in the route handler anyway.

This obviously isn't useful for everyone — plenty of apps have genuinely cross-cutting concerns that have to live in middleware. But for any app where body-forwarding routes can be moved out of middleware scope, it's strictly more reliable than the `req.clone()` pattern that #86373 documents as ineffective.

For 15.x users: the patch-package approach from #86373 also still works:

```diff
// node_modules/next/dist/server/next-server.js
} finally {
    if (hasRequestBody) {
-       requestData.body.finalize();
+       await requestData.body.finalize();
    }
}
```

