> This tells Next.js that the page should be statically rendered.
> 
> A helpful note: You might think setting `dynamic = "force-static"` would handle this, but it actually works a bit differently than you'd expect. Instead of controlling whether the page renders statically, it changes how functions like `await headers()` behave—they'll return empty objects rather than actual values. I'd generally recommend avoiding `"force-static"` since it's easy to misunderstand what it does, and you might not get the behavior you're looking for.

Why is the other behavior the default, and when would that ever be useful? If `generateStaticParams` returns an empty array, dynamic pages (that use `cookies()` or `await params` etc.) will still be dynamic so as far as I can tell there's no downside doing this.

The current default prevents routes with path segments from being prefetched as static pages. Changing the default would probably speed up a *lot* of the large set of "mostly-RCC" dashboards out there, going from 1 RTT when clicking a link to 0 with no code changes. (It certainly did speed up ours.)

More specifically, the kind of app where this is really useful is something like this:

```ts
// /[slug]/page.tsx
export function Page() {
  // notably, does NOT access `param`, `searchParams`, `cookies`, etc.
  <PageClient />
}

// /[slug]/page-client.tsx
"use client";
export function PageClient() {
  const pathname = usePathname();
  // parses the slug here 
}
```

Here, the page is currently dynamic, and hence it won't be cached & prefetched as aggressively. The server component, which is essentially empty, requires one extra RTT on every request.

(Generally I find the Next.js defaults to be quite hostile towards "app-dot" client-only websites, eg. by not being able to export metadata from a client component `page.tsx`. The advice I usually hear is that I should switch to other frameworks for client-only apps, but I like Next.js and IMO a small number of fixes could make it the best framework for all types of React apps.)

