`composes` compiles to a list of class names.

```css
@layer base {
  .paragraph {
    background-color: red;
    composes: paragraph from '../component-a/ComponentA.module.css';
  }
}
```

would create something like `export const paragraph = "componentB-paragraph-4723 componentA-paragraph-8824"`.

So the composes just adds the class name.

But it can't force it to be in layer `base`. It's just a class name and it depends on component A in which layer they are defined.

So we can't "set" the layer for `composes`, so it's kind of invalid to have it inside of a `@layer` block. In webpack (css-loader) it wasn't checked, but the `@layer` had no effect on `composes`. Basically silently ignoring the wrong scenario.

But ignoring that is not great. In the code above it looks like `parapgraph` from component a is in the `base` layer. But actually it might not. It depends on the declaration in component a.
But the point is that this code give you the potentially false assumption that it will put it into the `base` layer.

Instead this can be used:

```css
.paragraph {
  @layer base {
    background-color: red;
  }
  composes: paragraph from '../component-a/ComponentA.module.css';
}
```

Here it's clear that composes is not in a layer. You might look up component A to see it that puts it into a layer.

