Example work-around in `proxy.ts` (cf [doc](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/proxy)) in the meantime
```ts
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server';
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';

// Needed because of https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/85631
function sanitizeHeaders(response: NextResponse): NextResponse {
  for (const [key, value] of response.headers.entries()) {
    if (!/^[\x00-\x7F]*$/.test(value)) {
      response.headers.delete(key);
    }
  }

  return response;
}

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const response = NextResponse.next();
  return sanitizeHeaders(response);
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/*',
};

export default proxy;
```

This is an issue for application-side code for constant files with multiple exports too. 

Empirically, for the unused constants to be tree-shaken they need to be split into their own unique files so the CJS build output doesn't combine them all into one exported object which can't be properly tree-shaken.

If I have multiple exports in a file like `constants.ts`
```typescript
export const string1 = "string1";
export const string2 = "string2";
```
If I import one of them a page like `pages/blog.tsx`
```typescript
import { string1 } from './constants';
```
The resulting bundle for that import looks something like this after it's built and shipped to the client:

```javascript
68546: (e, s, t) => {
        "use strict";
        t.d(s, {
            Q: () => a,
            z: () => r
        });
        let a = "string1"
          , r = "string2"
    }
```
`string2` didn't get tree-shaken even though it should have. This issue is resulting in many more bytes being sent to the client of Next.js applications that use this pattern. This issue should get more attention.

The solution for now is to not export multiple things from the same file, but the bundler should be able to handle this.

