This really surprised me when I migrated from Webpack to Turbopack: I had Cypress tests that would fail because Cypress started interacting with the page before the forms had been filled out.

It's very tricky to reproduce this bug because it seems very dependent on chunk ordering (so I'm only seeing this issue on certain pages) and on very specific timing conditions that are not always consistent between browsers. I cannot, for example, reproduce this bug on Firefox using the built-in latency condition tester in the Network dev tools, because I've found in that browser that the React chunk always loads "in time" for early page interaction (at least, as fast as I can physically interact with the page).

The only foolproof solution I know of for this is to disable form fields (inputs, buttons that would by default trigger a form submit, etc) until the page has hydrated. This is relatively straightforward, if a pain because you have to apply this to all of your custom inputs:

```ts
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function useHydrated() {
  const [hydrated, setHydrated] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/set-state-in-effect
    setHydrated(true);
  }, []);

  return hydrated;
}

function MyInput() {
  const isHydrated = useHydrated();

  return (
    <input disabled={!isHydrated} />
  )
}
```

I've done this for now on the pages with failing tests as a stopgap. This may not be ideal if you use `disabled` to trigger clearly-visible styling changes with transitions (e.g. fading to or from a grayed-out state), otherwise I think it should be fine.

Whether this is truly a Turbopack or Next.js bug I'm not sure. Would React core team guidance be to always ensure React is early in the load order as possible to minimize the time to interactive? Did Turbopack intentionally or unintentionally make changes to that load order from Webpack? I would understand if from the perspective of either, the real answer here is supposed to be "disable everything during SSR that does not work without JavaScript being loaded;" but it's not a practice I've heard discussed or shared before.

Related scenario I ran into, FWIW - a submit button inside a form that normally `preventDefault`'d would execute native browser form submission (e.g. page reload) if the button was clicked before React hydrated. This makes sense; I don't know what other solutions there are beyond similarly using `disabled` (maybe some weird tricks around using a non-submit button, but I wouldn't want to impact accessibility).

Oh, one other note for anyone else who winds up with this issue due to automated testing - you can *probably* set up Cypress or Playwright to wait until the page is hydrated to actually start executing. I haven't investigated this yet, but it seems doable. That said, [Playwright advises against this](https://playwright.dev/docs/navigations#hydration).

