Hey everyone, wanted to provide some answers here around the specific issues brought up by @SorooshGb, as well as some more general info around why we bundled this change with Cache Components.

---

In general, to fix these types of issues, you'll need to make the component state you want to reset controlled, and then use either events or cleanup effects to actually reset it.

You can find the code for all the changes here: https://github.com/SorooshGb/next16-activity-breakage-repro/pull/1

**A. Dropdown stays open**

Start by making the dropdown menu controlled:

```tsx
export function ItemDropdown() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);

  return (
    <DropdownMenu open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>
      {/* ... */}
    </DropdownMenu>
  );
}

```

Now, you have two options. First, you could close the dropdown on each interaction that navigates away from the page. In this case, using the onNavigate callback from Link:

```tsx
<Link href={`/item/${i}`} onNavigate={() => setOpen(false)}>
  <EyeIcon />
</Link>

<Link href={`/item/${i}?newEntry=true`} onNavigate={() => setOpen(false)}>
  <PlusCircleIcon />
</Link>

```

The second option is to use a Layout Effect's cleanup function to reset the state if the component rerenders and the URL is not `/`:

```tsx
export function ItemDropdown() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      if (window.location.pathname !== '/') {
        setOpen(false);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  // ...
}

```

We want `useLayoutEffect` instead of `useEffect` here because conceptually the clean-up is tied to the component's UI being visually hidden. A regular effect could be delayed by something like a re-suspending component or View Transition.

**B. Dialog behavior breaks because mount-time logic never runs again**

I think the best option here is to use the search param as the source of truth for the dialog. You can use router.replace to not add history entries.

```tsx
export function EntryDialog() {
  const router = useRouter();
  const searchParams = useSearchParams();
  const open = searchParams.get('newEntry') === 'true';

  return (
    <Dialog
      open={open}
      onOpenChange={(open) => {
        router.replace(
          window.location.pathname + (open ? '?newEntry=true' : ''),
        );
      }}
    >
      {/* ... */}
    </Dialog>
  );
}

```

Lastly, make the New Item button render a Link that sets `?newEntry=true`:

```tsx
<DialogTrigger asChild>
  <Button asChild>
    <Link href={{ query: { newEntry: true } }} replace={true}>
      New Item <PlusIcon />
    </Link>
  </Button>
</DialogTrigger>

```

In prod there's no extra server-side roundtrips when toggling the dialog since both versions will be prefetched.

One nice thing about this approach is that if you have some DOM or React state in the New Item form (an `<input>` for example), start to fill it out but then press the browser Back button, if you go back to the page (either via the Add Item link or by pressing the browser Forward button), the state will be preserved.

**C. E2E test fails due to duplicate DOM**

Here you can use the recommended helpers `getByRole`, which already filter by visibility. Filtering by visibility is a general best practice since it’s robust to a variety of UI elements like tabbed navs or disclosures/accordions.

```tsx
await page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'name' }).fill('Test User');

await page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Email' }).fill(email);
await page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Password' }).fill(password);

```

With that change, the test passes.

---

Now let me try to add some color as to why we bundled this change with Cache Components.

A big motivation for the changes in Cache Components was to enable App Router apps to have instant navigations, like those found in traditional SPAs.

Traditional client-first SPAs (like Pages Router apps) have instant client-side navigations, but suffer from other problems the App Router was designed to solve: client-server waterfalls, lack of support for server-side rewrites and redirects, and other similar limitations that come from a client-first model.

So, if we wanted to keep all the benefits of server-driven routing, how could we speed up navigations in the App Router? The answer is prefetching.

If you can prefetch a server-rendered page, it can be available instantly once the user clicks a link. As soon as you allow a page to be rendered ahead of a navigation, though, you immediately face a problem: it could be stale by the time the user *actually* clicks the link.

Cache Components was designed in part to solve this problem. By giving parts of your UI a lifetime with "use cache", Next can now safely prefetch a server-rendered page ahead of time, and know whether it's stale once the user actually navigates, or if it's still valid and can be safely shown instantly from the client-side cache.

So, "use cache" lets you express that a dynamic page has a lifetime that extends beyond that page being currently active. That's how Cache Components can speed up Server Components. But what about Client Components?

Client Components have their own notion of short-lived state. Sometimes it's DOM state in the browser (uncontrolled form fields, scroll position), sometimes it's React state, and sometimes it's data the component suspended on with `use()`.

In pure multi-page apps (MPAs), the browser maintains this ephemeral state in the back/forward cache. That's why if you scroll a page and fill out a form in an MPA and submit it, then press the back button, you'll see an instant navigation to the previous page, with its scroll position and form data intact. In SPAs however, this behavior requires developers to manually persist and restore the ephemeral state themselves. This might involve hoisting all the state they want to preserve beyond unmounting to a parent context or external store; or frameworks may try to save scroll position alongside a history stack. And even then, DOM state from uncontrolled elements like form inputs and video players, or scroll positions of inner scrollable elements, won't be restored.

So, part of the motivation for adding Activity around route segments was to maintain this default browser behavior for *all* Next apps. Pressing back can instantly show the previous page with *all* its state intact, regardless of the particulars of how an app handles that state today.

But what about forward-looking cases? Just like "use cache" enables a prefetch of the RSC portion of an upcoming page, Activity enables a prerender of the client portion. Today, we don't actually prerender new pages with Activity, but if you've visited them before, new navigations will re-use them (which is the main subject of this current thread).

You may be wondering, why would we reuse an old history entry's client tree for a brand *new* history entry? Indeed, even pure MPAs don't do this.

A few reasons.

First, there are many cases where it improves the out-of-the-box UX. Preserving already-loaded images or data for forward navs without external caches, restoring scroll position when navigating back to a Home tab, and keeping draft form state warm all address a lot of longstanding issues that Next users have raised over the years. (Note that the current behavior in Cache Components is to keep the latest 3 routes rendered inside a hidden Activity. This is a heuristic which, while imperfect, tries to strike a balance between not bloating memory usage and keeping routes the user is most likely to navigate back to warm. If you wanted some ephemeral state to persist beyond this, you’d need to model and persist it explicitly, either in something like local storage or on the server in a database. So the Activity behavior should really be thought more of as a default for unmodeled ephemeral state, i.e., state that you either haven’t yet or can’t easily model yourself.)

But second, and more importantly, it forces apps using Cache Components to write client code that tolerates being hidden with Activity. As mentioned above, we don't currently prerender *new* upcoming navigations with Activity. But one day, that might become part of App Router’s behavior. Moreover, we have ongoing work around integrating View Transitions with page navigations, as well as bringing React's new Gesture APIs to Next. Both of these features (as well as others) rely on Activity-robust client code as part of their foundation, since animations really only work if the old and new screens are already ready to be displayed.

So, that's why the Activity change shipped alongside Cache Components. Cache Components give Server Components longer lifetimes so they can be prefetched and reused safely; Activity gives Client Components longer lifetimes so their state can persist and they can be prerendered. Together they lay the groundwork for fast, animated, resilient navigations in App Router apps.

---

All this being said, we know that this is new behavior, and in some cases may require significant refactoring to existing apps. We're really excited that everyone here wants to try Cache Components, and understand the frustration with not being able to because of the Activity-related changes.

So, we're considering some possible mitigation strategies that would let you take advantage of the features that exist in Cache Components today.

One strategy would be adding a way for folks to opt-out of Activity for specific route segments. Another would be to use it for backward navigations only.

These opt-outs would let you try out all the features of Cache Components today, but they wouldn't be compatible with new features, including the upcoming `instant` route segment config option that enables runtime prefetching of user-specific data. But, they would let you migrate to Cache Components sooner, and then gradually allow you to opt-in to more features as you ensure that your app’s client code is robust to Activity.

Again, just want to apologize for our lack of communication around this change. It's a big one and we have more educational work to do, but it's also foundational to a lot of really exciting features coming to Next. Our hope is that as more apps embrace the Activity changes instead of working against them, we'll see more examples of how to address the common problems, as well as the benefits the new model brings for keeping Next apps feeling as snappy as possible.

> Until there’s an official way to opt out of `<Activity>` per route segment, is it safe to rely on the experimental `use cache` and `ppr` flags in the meantime, or is there a risk those will be deprecated before an `<Activity>` opt-out is available?

@JesperCph The `ppr` flag was dropped in one of the 15 canaries, so your app will no longer build with it past that version. The `useCache` flag will likely exist for a bit since it can actually be helpful when migrating to cache components.

All that being said I expect we'll have a solution for Activity-related issues before making any new deprecations.

@MartinCura I had the same issue and Claude and I solved it like:

## Fix

Activity keeps cached route components in the DOM, so the `data-conversation-open` attribute never actually goes away when navigating back. The CSS selector keeps matching and the list stays hidden.

The fix is to swap the CSS-only approach for `usePathname()`, which always reflects the real current route regardless of what Activity has cached:

```tsx
"use client";

import { usePathname } from "next/navigation";

export function ChatVisibility({ list, detail }) {
  const pathname = usePathname();
  const isConversationOpen = /\/chat\/[^/]+/.test(pathname);

  return (
    <>
      <div className={isConversationOpen ? "max-md:hidden" : ""}>{list}</div>
      <div className={isConversationOpen ? "" : "max-md:hidden"}>{detail}</div>
    </>
  );
}
```

Basically: don't rely on DOM presence for layout visibility when Activity is enabled — use router state instead.
