html:has([data-hide="true"]:not([style*="display: none !important"] *)) {
    ...
}
```

@MartinCura Absolutely agree that CSS + DOM is sometimes the simplest and best solution to a problem, just want to point out that if you go that approach React is no longer "aware" of what's rendered/mounted/visible and what isn't. So there may be some features like animations or lower-priority updates that those parts of your UI wouldn't be able to take advantage of.

For the conditional button in a navbar based on the current page, I think parallel routes are the idiomatic API for accomplishing that. And again we are designing features like ViewTransitions across routes to play nicely with them—think of iOS's navbar that animates titles and back buttons in and out while gesturing or transitioning across pages.

