What a rabbit hole, here's what I discovered:

- To find the download URL of SWC, Next.js call `getRegistry` in `packages/next/src/lib/download-swc.ts`
- `getRegistry` calls `getPkgManager` from `packages/next/src/lib/helpers/get-pkg-manager.ts`, which returns `pnpm`
- `getRegistry` then execute `pnpm config get registry`

This command worked for pnpm v10.7.0 and prior versions. In v10.7.1, they modified how `pnpm get config` works by fallbacking to npm for certain config keys (see `settingShouldFallBackToNpm`).

https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm/pull/9346/files

This means that Next.js is effectively running `npm config get registry` for pnpm v10.7.1+. This is fine in most cases, but not for workspaces as the `--no-workspaces` flag is missing.

This is already documented in `getRegistry`:

```
// Since `npm config` command fails in npm workspace to prevent workspace config conflicts,
// add `--no-workspaces` flag to run under the context of the root project only.
// Safe for non-workspace projects as it's equivalent to default `--workspaces=false`.
// x-ref: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/47121#issuecomment-1499044345
// x-ref: https://github.com/npm/statusboard/issues/371#issue-920669998
```

Knowing that pnpm already defaults to npm, I wonder if `pkgManager` should be set to `npm` if `getPkgManager` returns `pnpm`. By doing so, the flag would be applied and the command would work.

