Thanks for reporting this! What you're experiencing is expected behavior with Cache Components. Let me explain what's happening and how to achieve the caching behavior you want.

### Why This Happens

When Cache Components is enabled, all routes are dynamic by default. For your `/posts/[id]` route, Next.js doesn't know at build time whether your page component uses runtime APIs like `cookies()` or `headers()`. When you have a `loading.tsx` file, it automatically wraps your page in a Suspense boundary, making the loading state the "static shell" that gets rendered.

### Important Limitation

**You cannot have both streaming (Suspense) and ISR caching.** This is a fundamental architectural constraint - you must choose between:
- Static/ISR caching (no streaming, full page cached)
- Dynamic rendering with streaming (always shows loading states, no ISR cache)

### Solutions

You have two options depending on your use case:

**Option 1: Static Rendering with ISR (Recommended for cacheable content)**

If your blog posts don't depend on runtime APIs, you can enable static rendering by exporting `generateStaticParams`:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  // You can return actual slugs from your CMS/database
  // or just one example to prove static renderability
  return [
    { slug: 'my-first-post' },
    { slug: 'another-post' }
  ]
}

export default async function BlogPost({ params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)
  return <Article post={post} />
}
```

This proves to Next.js that your page can be statically rendered, allowing it to cache the actual content instead of just the loading state. The page will be ISR cached and won't stream.

**Option 2: Dynamic Rendering with Streaming (No ISR Cache)**

If you need runtime APIs (e.g., checking user authentication) or want streaming behavior, wrap the dynamic parts in Suspense:

```tsx
// app/posts/[id]/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'

async function BlogContent({ id }: { id: string }) {
  // This component might use cookies() or other runtime APIs
  const post = await getPost(id)
  return <Article post={post} />
}

export default async function BlogPost({ params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }) {
  const { id } = await params
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<Loading />}>
      <BlogContent id={id} />
    </Suspense>
  )
}
```

### Trade-offs

- **Option 1**: Full ISR caching, no loading states on cached pages, but no streaming
- **Option 2**: Streaming capability, always shows loading states, no ISR caching

For a blog, Option 1 is typically the best choice as blog posts are usually static content that benefits more from caching than streaming.

Check out the [Cache Components documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/cache-components) for more details on this behavior.

You also might be interested in my talk I gave in London this year that covers this topic specifically https://gitnation.com/contents/from-segments-to-suspense-the-future-of-nextjs-caching

