> This could be done in a shorter form, but there's a bug https://github.com/facebook/react/issues/35340

The pattern is basically, not needing to await the work upfront. RSC and its protocols can serialize a promise over the wire.

You'd likely want this on a layout, but for example sake, I put it in the page here.

```tsx
import { headers } from "next/headers";
import { Provider } from "./providers";
import { Menu } from "./menu";

const sleep = async (duration: number) =>
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, duration));

async function loadUserAgent() {
  const headersList = await headers();
  const userAgent = headersList.get("user-agent") ?? "unknown user-agent";

  await sleep(1000);

  return userAgent;
}

export default async function Page() {
  const data = loadUserAgent();

  return (
    <Provider data={data}>
      <h1>Hello there</h1>
      <Menu />
    </Provider>
  );
}
```

The you can move the Suspense boundary to read the data from the promise, way down the tree, and skip the root loading.tsx from showing in this case.

