This still not a React 19 or Next.js bug - it's a dependency array issue causing an infinite update loop.

You have, https://github.com/jpoindexter/nextjs-useLayoutEffect-state-reset/blob/2cd6f8f7dc01489e2013b6a121fca3b976876a2f/contexts/right-rail-context.tsx#L12-L19:

```tsx
// Provider creates a NEW object on every render
<RightRailContext.Provider value={{ content, setContent }}>
```

And https://github.com/jpoindexter/nextjs-useLayoutEffect-state-reset/blob/2cd6f8f7dc01489e2013b6a121fca3b976876a2f/contexts/right-rail-context.tsx#L33-L41:

```tsx
// Hook depends on that unstable reference
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  context?.setContent(content);
  return () => context?.setContent(null);
}, [context, content, updateKey]);  // ← `context` changes every render
```

When `setContent` is called:

1. Provider re-renders → creates new `{ content, setContent }` object
2. `context` reference changes → effect cleanup runs → calls `setContent(null)`
3. Provider re-renders again → goto 1

So even if you did `useCallback` around the `setContent` function, the Context Provider value itself is recreated. In fact, `useState`'s setter function is guaranteed to be stable, so wrapping it in `useCallback` is useless/un-necessary.

This causes "Maximum update depth exceeded" - a classic infinite loop from unstable dependencies.

**_Even_** if you memoize the context value so the object reference stays stable, upon calling `setContent`, you'd be creating a new `value` and we are back to the `When setContent is called` list:

```tsx
export function RightRailProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  const [content, setContent] = useState<ReactNode>(null);
 // ⚠️ this would get recreated when setContent is called anyway
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ content, setContent }), [content]);
  return (
    <RightRailContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </RightRailContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

A solution is this nice thing they have at the React docs, with a double context: https://react.dev/learn/scaling-up-with-reducer-and-context#step-2-put-state-and-dispatch-into-context

```tsx
export function RightRailProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  const [content, setContent] = useState<ReactNode>(null);

  return (
    <RightRailContentContext.Provider value={content}>
      <RightRailDispatchContext.Provider value={setContent}>
        {children}
      </RightRailDispatchContext.Provider>
    </RightRailContentContext.Provider>
  );
}

export function useRightRailContent() {                                                                                                                                
    return useContext(RightRailContentContext);                                                                                                                          
}                                                                                                                                                                      

export function useSetRightRail(content: ReactNode, updateKey?: unknown) {                                                                                             
  const setContent = useContext(RightRailDispatchContext);                                                                                                             

  useLayoutEffect(() => {                                                                                                                                              
    setContent?.(content);                                                                                                                                             
    return () => setContent?.(null);                                                                                                                                   
  }, [setContent, content, updateKey]);                                                                                                                                
}                                                                                                                                                                             
```

Now a component exclusively calling `setContent`, for example though `useSetRightRail`,  doesn't have to re-render when `content` changes.

