This is a regression in how Next.js 15.4/15.5 resolves the default locale when a domains array is present in the i18n config.

The behavior change: in v15.3, when browsing on the default domain, locale detection uses the main defaultLocale from the i18n config. In v15.4+, if a locale also appears in a domain's config (e.g. "fr" exists in both the default domain and "example.fr"), Next.js incorrectly picks up the domain's defaultLocale instead.

You mentioned the correct value is still present in `req[Symbol.for("NextInternalRequestMeta")]` but its not being used. So the locale resolution logic changed to prefer the domain-level default over the request-level detection.

Workaround: handle this in your middleware by checking the actual host and overriding the locale:

```ts
export function middleware(request) {
  const host = request.headers.get("host");
  const isDefaultDomain = host === "yourdomain.com";

  if (isDefaultDomain) {
    const url = request.nextUrl.clone();
    // force correct locale for default domain
    return NextResponse.rewrite(url);
  }
}
```

Or if you dont actually need domain-based locale routing (i.e. you support all locales on all domains), consider removing the domains array from the i18n config and handling domain routing separately. That avoids the matching issue entirely.

