This looks like a bug in the bundled version of postcss-preset-env that Next.js ships internally. The `@csstools/postcss-media-minmax` plugin that transpiles CSS Media Queries Level 4 range syntax is dropping the outer parentheses during transformation.

The input uses modern range syntax:

```css
@media (width >= 768px) and (width < 1280px) {
  .d-only-tablet-none { display: none; }
}
```

And the transpiled output loses a closing bracket, producing invalid CSS the browser cant parse.

Two workarounds:

Override the bundled postcss-preset-env by installing a newer version:

```bash
yarn add postcss-preset-env@latest
```

Or disable the media-query-ranges feature in postcss-preset-env if you only support modern browsers anyway:

```js
export default {
  plugins: {
    'postcss-preset-env': {
      features: {
        'media-query-ranges': false,
      },
    },
  },
};
```

You could also pre-compile the CSS before importing it, using the legacy media query syntax directly in your source.

