This is tricky. The core issue is that importing next/headers at the module level (even transitively, even if headers() is never called) during next.config.ts resolution poisons the AsyncLocalStorage context.

In Next.js 16, the next/headers module eagerly initializes internal stores when imported. When this happens during config resolution (which runs outside the normal request lifecycle), the stores get set up in the wrong context. Later when actual page renders run, they find a stale/mismatched store and throw the Expected workUnitAsyncStorage error.

The fact that you cant reproduce it in minimal setups suggests it depends on pnpm workspace module resolution hoisting. In complex monorepos, pnpm might resolve next/headers to a shared instance that gets loaded once and persists across both config and runtime contexts.

Workarounds:

Avoid importing modules that transitively depend on next/headers in next.config.ts. Keep config imports separate from runtime imports.

Or use dynamic import with type-only static import:

```ts
// next.config.ts
import type { ConfigClient } from "./client";

export default async function config() {
  const { client } = await import("./client");
  // use client here
}
```

You could also split your barrel exports so the next/headers-dependent code is in a separate entry that config files dont touch.

This looks like a Node.js 24.14+ regression in how undici handles request body consumption on error responses.

When you pass a Request object to fetch() and the server returns a 4xx/5xx, Node's patched fetch tries to read the response body but the original request body source has already been consumed during the request lifecycle. This didnt happen in older Node versions because body source handling was different.

Key detail: it only happens when you pass a Request object. Using plain options works fine:

```ts
// crashes on Node >= 24.14.0
const req = new Request(url, { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify({ test: true }) });
const res = await fetch(req);

// works fine
const res = await fetch(url, {
  method: "POST",
  body: JSON.stringify({ test: true }),
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" }
});
```

Workaround: either avoid passing Request objects directly and use URL + options instead, or clone the request before passing it:

```ts
const res = await fetch(req.clone());
```

.clone() creates a fresh copy with its own body stream which avoids the consumption issue. Might be worth opening an issue on the Node.js side too since the underlying change is in undici's request body handling.

