        },
        "./next/pagesDir": {
            "module": "./esm/next/pagesDir.js",
            "require": "./next/pagesDir.js"
        },
       ...
```

Then I no longer see the error, ie, only ESM loads, and the CSS is displayed correctly.


Similarly, if I modify turbopack [`turbopack-core/src/resolve/remap.rs`](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/turbopack/crates/turbopack-core/src/resolve/remap.rs#L149) so that it prefers `module` over `default` like so: 

```rust
                let has_module = list.iter().any(|(condition, _)| condition == "module");
                for (condition, value) in list {
                    if condition == "default" && has_module {
                        continue;
                    }
                ...
```

Then I also no longer see the error. 

What also works is modifying [`turbopack-core/src/resolve/node.rs`](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/turbopack/crates/turbopack-core/src/resolve/node.rs#L44) by adding `module` to the list

```rust
pub fn node_esm_resolve_options(root: FileSystemPath) -> Vc<ResolveOptions> {
    let conditions: ResolutionConditions = [
        (rcstr!("node"), ConditionValue::Set),
        (rcstr!("import"), ConditionValue::Set),
        (rcstr!("module"), ConditionValue::Set), // New line added
    ]
    .into();
```

However, I'm not sure if this would have additional problems. NodeJS does not specify `module` in it's documentation on [conditional  exports](https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html#conditional-exports). [Webpack does reference it though](https://webpack.js.org/guides/package-exports/#conditions) and, I'm not entirely sure, but it seems correct. 

