Say we have...

- `app/form/page.tsx` plain `<form method="post" action="/form/confirm">`
- `app/form/confirm/route.ts` which receives a `POST` req, to read the body, then **internally fetches** the rendered page with the data in a header, and streams the HTML back. `GET` just `redirect('/form')` so refresh/direct hits don't 405.
- `app/form/confirm/view/page.tsx` the actual UI. Reads the forwarded body via `headers()`. It has to live one segment deeper because `route.ts` + `page.tsx` conflict at the same segment...
- An self signed short-lived token in `x-form-proxy` so direct hits to `/form/confirm/view` get rejected and redirected to `/form`.
- `/form/verify` which uses the same proxy trick for OTP failure: re-render the confirm view with the email + error preserved; on success, just redirect.

```ts
// app/form/confirm/route.ts
export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  const formData = await request.formData();

  const payload: Record<string, string> = {};
  for (const [k, v] of formData.entries()) {
    if (typeof v === "string") payload[k] = v;
  }

  return fetch(new URL("/form/confirm/view", request.url), {
    headers: {
      "accept-encoding": "identity",
      [PROXY_HEADER]: sign(),
      [FORWARD_HEADER]: JSON.stringify(payload),
      cookie: request.headers.get("cookie") ?? "",
    },
  });

}

export async function GET() { redirect("/form"); }
```

