Symptom: after on-disk file churn while the dev server is running (most reliably a git switch/branch checkout touching many files), an incremental rebuild serves CSS that is missing every class contributed via @source — the dashboard visually collapses (no flex, no spacing, no borders). Lint/typecheck/next build all stay green; production is unaffected.

Verified server-side, not just browser cache: we curled the compiled CSS chunk and grepped for the missing utilities — present after a fresh build, absent after the churn-triggered incremental rebuild. (The browser-cache angle makes it worse though: the dev CSS chunk filename doesn't change, so after recovering you also need a hard refresh.)

Recovery: kill the server, rm -rf .next, restart, hard-refresh. next dev --webpack doesn't exhibit it.

This fits the mechanism described here: files matched by @source are read by @tailwindcss/postcss but aren't module-graph dependencies, so if the PostCSS transform serves a stale/cached compile (the mtime-vs-content race in the OP), the rescan of @source files never happens and their classes vanish wholesale rather than lagging one revision.

